  "summary": "Five recent items probe progenitor fate and tissue organization across mouse, planarian, zebrafish and organoid models, from lineage tracing to Wnt and SOX9 programs.",
  "synthesis": "The items share a focus on how progenitor populations are maintained and allocated to fates during early development, across diverse systems. Whole-embryo barcoding with scRNA-seq indicates mouse mesoderm clones diverge yet overlap extensively, favoring convergent origins over simple lineage hierarchies. In planarians, canonical Wnt signaling governs early proliferation and neural, muscle and parenchymal differentiation, with anterior and posterior tips acting as organizers. A SOX9-high radial glia program, regulated by Notch and SOX9, links progenitor maintenance to neuronal identity. Complementary resources include a whole-mount 3D quantitative platform for dorsal forebrain spheroids, which detects growth and rosette phenotypes in an isogenic Down syndrome model, and a zebrafish meninges atlas implicating Foxc1 and Wnt. The systems are heterogeneous; the common thread is signaling-regulated progenitor behavior.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-19"
